course: dictionary
lesson: 7761
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and context tells you which one is right. The word purge can mean to clean out completely, to remove people who are not wanted, or to make someone free from sin. The words around it are your clues.
explain: «Purge the pipes» means clean them out, so this is about cleaning, not people. Test the meaning in the sentence. "Clean out the pipes" makes sense, but "remove the pipes from sin" does not.
explain: In history, a purge is a time when a leader or government removes or punishes lots of people, often harshly. Clues such as «the secret police arrested huge numbers of people» show that this is a cruel removal, not a tidy-up. Think about the feeling of the word as well as its dictionary meaning.
fact: Context means the words and sentences around a word.
fact: «Purge» can be a verb (to purge) or a noun (a purge).
fact: «Purge» means to get rid of people, things or ideas that are not wanted, often harshly.
fact: In history, «a purge» is a time when a government or leader removes or punishes lots of people; the Great Terror in the Soviet Union in 1937–1938 is also called the purges.
fact: «Purge» can also mean to clean out completely, as in «purge old files from the computer», and in church it means to cleanse the soul from sin.
exercise: «The ruler purged his enemies from the army.» What does purged mean here? | Cleaned the army's boots | Taught them to sing | *Removed them, often harshly | Gave them a medal for their hard work
exercise: Dad says he will purge the old files from the computer. What does purge mean here? | To make a cake | *To clean out completely | To punish a government | To shout loudly
exercise: «In 1937–1938 the Soviet secret police arrested huge numbers of people.» Which clue shows this was a big removal of people? | *huge numbers | Soviet | USSR | 1937
test: «Purge the pipes» is a clue that purge means what? | To make someone free from sin | To remove people harshly | *To clean out completely | To empty with strong medicine
test: In «the purges under Stalin», purges is a noun. What does it mean? | *Times when a government removes lots of people | Cleanings of pipes | Church services | Strong medicines
exam: Which phrase tells you that purge means a clear-out, not a cruel removal of people? | His enemies | *Old files from the computer | The ruler | Removed them from the army
